 "backlog": [
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "data",
   "id": "b036",
   "spec": "Use calculated logP as a physics prior. In fit_predict, compute c = Crippen.MolLogP for each train and test molecule (from rdkit.Chem import Crippen; cache per SMILES like the descriptors). Fit a 1-D Huber regression (sklearn HuberRegressor) of y on c over the training set to get a, b, and set the baseline to base = a*c + b. Run the whole existing pipeline (OOF stacking, base_predict, meta) on the residual target r = y - base_tr instead of y, then return meta.predict(base_te) + base_te_prior, where base_te_prior = a*c_te + b. Features are unchanged.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Residual learning on Crippen logP"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "architecture",
   "id": "b040",
   "spec": "Add a nearest-neighbour base learner that uses the existing Tanimoto matrix. In base_predict, compute S = tanimoto(Fb, Fa) (already available as a helper, and it is cheap). For each row of Xb, take the k=7 most similar training molecules by S, and predict the similarity-weighted mean of ya over those neighbours, with weights S**2 and a small epsilon in the denominator. Return this as an extra column, and update the oof column count in fit_predict from 3 to 4 accordingly. Use argsort on -S, or np.argpartition for speed. This gives the stacker a local-similarity signal that differs from the kernel ridge fit.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Tanimoto kNN regressor as a base learner"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "architecture",
   "id": "b044",
   "spec": "In base_predict, add a fourth base learner: catboost.CatBoostRegressor(iterations=800, learning_rate=0.05, depth=6, loss_function='MAE', rsm=0.2, random_seed=seed, thread_count=8, verbose=0), fit on Xa, ya and predict Xb. Append p_cb as a fourth column of the returned matrix, and change oof = np.zeros((len(y), 3)) to np.zeros((len(y), 4)). The positive Ridge meta-learner stays. If runtime gets near the limit, reduce iterations to 500.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Add CatBoost as fourth base learner"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "data",
   "id": "b048",
   "spec": "The descriptor block feeding the RBF kernel is currently only mean/std standardised, so heavy-tailed columns such as molecular weight, Ipc and various counts dominate Euclidean distances. In base_predict, replace that standardisation with sklearn.preprocessing.QuantileTransformer(n_quantiles=min(1000, n_train), output_distribution='normal', random_state=seed), fitted on the training descriptor rows and applied to both train and test rows. Afterwards drop any column whose standard deviation is below 1e-9, and keep gamma = 1.0 / (2 * n_remaining_columns). Only the kernel member is affected; the random forest and LightGBM members keep the existing clipped raw descriptors, and the stacking structure is unchanged.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Rank-Gauss transform of descriptors before the RBF kernel"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "optimizer",
   "id": "b052",
   "spec": "Change the LightGBM objective from regression_l1 to huber with alpha (the delta parameter) set to about 0.5, since logD targets have a range of roughly -1.5 to 4.5 and outliers are common. In lgb_params, set objective='huber' and add alpha=0.5. Huber gives smooth gradients near zero and robustness to outliers. Keep all other settings the same. Also try a second variant with objective='fair' and fair_c=0.5 if the first is not clearly better, but ship only the better one.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Huber objective for the LightGBM learner"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "data",
   "id": "b056",
   "spec": "Before training, clip the training targets to their 1st and 99th percentiles computed on train_y: y_c = np.clip(y, np.percentile(y, 1), np.percentile(y, 99)). Use y_c everywhere y is used inside fit_predict, including the oof loop and the final base_predict call. Test predictions are not clipped. This is a single line added after y is cast to float64.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Winsorise extreme training targets"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "data",
   "id": "b060",
   "spec": "Change the settings to N_BITS = 2048 and RADIUS = 3. Since the code slices descriptors as Xa[:, 2*N_BITS:] and the kernel uses Ftr[:, :N_BITS], those slices update automatically. Larger substructures and fewer bit collisions should help both the Tanimoto KRR and the trees. Make no other change. If runtime is too long, lower RF n_estimators from 200 to 150.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Radius-3 ECFP with 2048 bits"
  }
 ],
